## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern identified: **Timeout enforcement** + **ThreadAbortException as control flow**

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is removed. You must switch to cooperative cancellation.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        // CancellationTokenSource is IDisposable — always dispose it
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        Task<T> task = Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Prefer this async version in new code
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token)
                             .WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested
                                                  && !externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### What changed and why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` | Uses the thread pool; avoids manual thread management |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `Task.WaitAsync(token)` | Cancellation-aware waiting (.NET 6+ API) |
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` (via timeout) | Cooperative cancellation — no `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | The exception type thrown by cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely | No equivalent needed; cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow |

### ⚠️ Critical pitfall: the work won't stop by itself

The **fundamental behavior change** is that `Thread.Abort()` forcibly killed the thread mid-execution, but `CancellationToken` is **cooperative** — if `work()` doesn't check the token, it keeps running in the background even after the timeout fires.

If your `work` delegate does long-running or loopable work, you **must** thread the token through:

```csharp
// BEFORE: would be killed mid-loop by Thread.Abort
Func<int> work = () => {
    int sum = 0;
    foreach (var item in hugeList)
        sum += Process(item);
    return sum;
};

// AFTER: must cooperate with cancellation
Func<int> work = () => {
    int sum = 0;
    foreach (var item in hugeList)
    {
        cts.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint!
        sum += Process(item);
    }
    return sum;
};
```

### Other pitfalls to watch for

1. **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaks internal timers. Always `using`.
2. **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** — let it propagate; only catch at the orchestration boundary.
3. **Blocking I/O won't be interrupted** — `Thread.Sleep`, `socket.Read`, etc. ignore the token. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O with token overloads.
4. **Uncooperative third-party code** — if you can't modify the work to check a token, the only true equivalent to `Thread.Abort` is running it in a **separate process** and calling `Process.Kill()`.
